= Outlines

== Initial
+ Two takes on the aesthetic attitude - Nguyen and Noë.
  + How do each of our philosophers spell out their attitudes?
    + Nguyen as a limited conception of aesthetics as striving play
      toward a particular goal. His usage is limited to gameplay and
      e.g.~corporate life. What Husserl would term vocational. Nguyen’s
      conception is restricted to rule-following attitudes.
    + Noë brings the aesthetic attitude to a global scale. If Nguyen
      describes gaming, Noë describes metagaming. His claim (a
      provocative one) is that the aesthetic attitude is essential to
      our Being and is what underscores the fact that we are problems to
      ourselves. Our meta-level contemplation is essential. But the
      superstructure modifies the base in what he terms the
      entanglement. We are wrapped up in habits and traditions of which
      we are aware and which we are free to break with and thus to
      modify. Our particular habits are defined by style, a particular
      way of going about our activities. Styles change over time against
      a background of tradition through a process of meta-level
      understanding and base-level modification.
    + Ironically, in Strange Tools, Noë argues that sports cannot be games.
      Nguyen, in Games: Agency as Art, decries this and shows how games can indeed
      by art. The idea is that Noë’s conception of what baseball players are doing
      is limited. Nguyen introduces the idea of striving play, that is, playing
      for the sake of the game, as a kind of uptake of the aesthetic attitude. It
      is this attitude that turns gameplay into a kind of artistic activity. I
      will argue that gameplay is in fact one of the most important aesthetic
      activities. The various characteristics of gameplay provide a clear
      articulation of what it means to take up the aesthetic attitude and provides
      a wealth of examples of it use. In particular, the practices of gaming and
      metagaming will prove fruitful for our analysis as a parallel between Noë’s
      base and meta-level activities. I will show that gaming and metagaming
      provide all the necessary features of the entanglement and provide a
      framework and rich literature for understanding the aesthetic attitude.
  + What are the tasks of the aesthetic attitude? (Positive and
    negative)
+ Varieties of ekstasis
  + What is ecstasy and why does it matter?
    + Ecstasy (ekstasis) is literally defined as being away from
      oneself. This is precisely the vocational bracketing that Husserl
      describes. Interestingly, it has connotations of pleasure. This
      may relate to the game-playing attitude which underlies it.
  + Sex and psychoanalysis - drive (libido)
    + Focus, flow-state, sexual desire and the libido. Draw on Lacan
      here, possibly D&G’s desiring machines. Social and individual
      investment are not the same: they are entangled. D&G go to far in
      equating the two.
  + Contemplation - philosophy and the arts
    + This is Noë’s whole shit. Draw heavily from The Entanglement and
      Strange Tools.
  + Code-switching and speech
    + Code-switching is when you take on the aesthetic attitude of one
      group for a time, then switch to another. This shows itself in
      speech, for example Black interpersonal speech as opposed to
      formal interracial speech (especially towards White folks).
      Similarly, code-switching from an autistic perspective to an
      allistic perspective (masking), or code-switching from one gender
      expression to another (gender fluidity) (e.g. literally
      genderfluid people, also drag). These exhibit a _multitude_
      of aesthetic attitudes and demonstrate the flexibility that we
      have in choosing our attitude. The fluidity by which one can
      code-switch is a kind of skill. And, as in Noë’s conception, once
      you know how to speak in one dialect you never really un-learn it.
      (Wittgenstein’s know-how vs know-that -- you never un-learn how to
      ride a bike.)
    + Potential citations: Autism, sexuality, and BDSM. Something on
      code-switching in Black speech. Code-switching in gender identity
      (Noë’s point on Butler’s conception of gender as performance: it
      is an aesthetic attitude toward our sexed beings).
  + Scientific pursuit
    + Scientific pursuit is a particular attitude defined by
      mathematical rigor and a material-reductionist approach to
      studying the natural world. Noë, toward the end of The
      Entanglement.
    + Counter D&G here: science as non-fundamental compared to the
      aesthetic attitude: it is an _application_ of the aesthetic
      attitude and not a fundamental orientation. Our fundamental
      orientation, insofar as we have one, is to be flexible.
+ Play and the magic circle: the shared characteristic of all ekstasis
  + The magic circle is defined as the 'realm of play;' to use Husserl’s
    terms it is the vocational attitude applied to a particular task.
    What makes a task a game rather than work is something to do with
    the motivations and benefits of pursuing that task. In any case the
    task pursued is part of the aesthetic attitude.
  + Flirtation, consensual sex, speech and language games, code
    switching, and gameplay all exhibit the aesthetic attitude - a way
    of changing our Being-in-the-world, and as such a way of
    _examining_ our Being. Contemplation is the meta-level act of
    understanding our own understandings and considering what could
    happen if those understandings changed. This cannot happen without
    play.
  + Play has several senses. In the first sense I intend play as it is
    typical understood in gameplay. Gameplay is a manner of
    rule-following. It is what happens when we take up the aesthetic
    attitude, the vocational attitude as it were. Another sense of the
    world 'play,' and the one I want to focus on here, can be defined as
    a loose experimentation where the boundaries of one’s subject and
    the rules of the game are up for interpretation. This can be
    considered a form of meta-gaming.
    + In #emph[Rules of Play,] Salem and Zimmerman propose three
      definitions. From lowest to highest abstraction, these are game
      play, ludic activity, and "being playful" or the "spirit of play."
      (p303). In general, play is defined as "free movement within a more
      rigid structure" (304).
    + 👉 There are many kinds of play, but the one I want to focus on is
      #strong[transformative play.] This "occurs when the free movment
      of play alters the more rigid structure in which it takes shape."
      (311). This is clearly parallel to Noë’s concept of the
      entanglement. Not all play has this entangled characteristic. It
      is transformative play in particular which is at the heart of
      entanglement and the aesthetic attitude.#footnote[They cite James
        S. Hans: "The role of play is not to work comfortably within its
        own structures but rather constantly to develop its structures
        through play." This is exactly what I’m trying to articulate!
        #emph[The Play of the World,] 1981, p.~5]
  + Metagaming and code-switching: the fluidity of the aesthetic
    attitude. DID as a disordered form of its extremity. Antisocial
    personality disorder and some expressions of autism as playing the
    game incorrectly, either as cheater, spoilsport, or non-participant.
    - On thinking like a designer.
  + Normalcy is just another game. The 'natural attitude' that Husserl
    describes is not so much a background predisposition as it is a
    learned habitus that we use in certain scenarios, e.g.~at home, at
    work, at the doctor’s office. Each of these locations has their own
    game to play and their own attitude to take up. There is no
    underlying basic habitus, only a 'default' which could well be
    anything. (Perhaps Nguyen has something to say here in The Score)
  + TO EXPLORE: *The hermeneutic circle and the magic circle - what's the difference?*
    - Okay clearly the magic circle more closely relates to Hans' 'fields of play' than the hermeneutic circle, but both are significant. The hermeneutic circle is closer to the entanglement but specifically for language. cf Hans p105
